WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.319 --> 00:00:04.500
I'm Alona Fyshe. I'm a professor at the
University of Alberta and I'm also a

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.599
fellow of the Alberta Machine
Intelligence Institute and I'm gonna

00:00:09.599 --> 00:00:12.660
follow in Jessica's footsteps in that
I'm not going to talk about exploration

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:16.830
of the outwards, but rather exploration
of inwards and how we know

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:22.261
what we know and how we could tell if a computer knew something too.

00:00:22.261 --> 00:00:26.200
so you've probably interacted with a Siri, or somebody like Siri,

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:27.869
either on your phone or maybe you

00:00:27.869 --> 00:00:29.799
have an Amazon Alexa in your house.

00:00:29.799 --> 00:00:34.000
I have had the experience of saying "hey Siri, call my mom."

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.260
And she does actually
place a call to my mother.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:40.365
But do you think that Siri understands the concept of mother?

00:00:40.365 --> 00:00:43.000
Do you think she understands the concept of call?

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.500
Even though she's a phone, I think she doesn't understand what even call means.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:51.570
So I'm interested in asking how do computers

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:56.160
understand meaning and language, and how
could we tell if they understood what we said?

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:02.219
So how do we test understanding?
If I was trying to test whether a person

00:01:02.219 --> 00:01:04.949
understood what I was saying I would ask
them to do something and if they did the

00:01:04.949 --> 00:01:07.860
right thing then I could say "oh they
understand me." But we've already decided

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:11.909
that that's not how we want to test Siri
Siri does what we asked her to do but we

00:01:11.909 --> 00:01:16.229
still don't believe that she understands
us. So let's think about how we test

00:01:16.229 --> 00:01:19.560
students we test students every day and
we do things

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:24.150
ask them about things in their world
things that we've taught them to see if

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:30.020
they know what what they've learned. So one thing that we can do to test

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:34.070
students about what what words mean is
to do analogy tests.

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:37.100
So, hammer is to nail as screwdriver is to...

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:44.000
Screw. Very good! Excellent work.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.000
Hammer is to nail as screwdriver is to screw.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.180
You're right. So this
tells us that you understand the

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:51.030
individual words and also the
relationships between the words and so

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:54.960
do you think we could get a computer to
do this task? That's the first

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.380
thing I'm going to talk about today.
We're going to use, in order to solve

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.740
analogies, we're going to use computer
models of word meaning.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.820
Computer models of word meaning are built on the
fact that words that are similar are

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:09.599
used in similar ways. So if we were to
look through all of the websites on the

00:02:09.599 --> 00:02:13.110
internet and look for the word "car" and
the word "truck," they would probably also

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:15.510
both be used in similar contexts. They would both be used

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:20.000
with the words "gas," "drive," "door," "steer,"
etcetera.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:21.900
That would tell us that they have similar meaning

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:24.750
What computer models of word meaning do is

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:28.650
to use that, leverage that fact in order
to build models. We're going to

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:32.519
calculate statistics about the word
co-occurrence, when two words occur together,

00:02:32.519 --> 00:02:36.780
and we're going to turn those
statistics into lists of numbers.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:40.500
When words have similar meaning, they will
have similar lists.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:45.120
Here I'm representing the numbers with the color
blue and if it's more blue than it's a

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.750
larger number and so these two words, "car"
and "truck," I've made a short list here

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:52.650
and because car and truck are similar
they have similar lists of numbers, but

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:56.700
they're not exactly the same. So we can
use this idea of lists of numbers in

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:02.340
order to tell if two words are similar.
So hammer is to nail as screwdriver is to

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:05.700
screw. now we need to use these lists of
numbers to actually perform the analogy

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:09.329
test. So we're actually going to do is
literally do math on these lists of

00:03:09.329 --> 00:03:13.230
numbers so we're gonna take the list
for nail, and subtract the list for

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:16.650
hammer. And add the list for screwdriver,
just element by element, and that gives

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:20.669
us a new vector, sorry a new list of numbers, and that new list of

00:03:20.669 --> 00:03:25.609
numbers tends to be close to the correct
answer to the analogy task. So this

00:03:25.609 --> 00:03:29.699
hypothetical list of numbers is close to
the co-occurent statistics we see for the

00:03:29.699 --> 00:03:33.355
word "screw." So let's test our computer
model.

00:03:33.355 --> 00:03:36.000
We're going to do the analogy test

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.739
where we choose from five options. So
if we were a randomly guessing, if you

00:03:40.739 --> 00:03:45.180
didn't know what you were doing, you
would get 20% on this particular test.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:50.629
The average US college applicant gets
57%, so better than guessing.

00:03:50.629 --> 00:03:54.599
It turns out that these word lists that I
just told you about actually do almost

00:03:54.599 --> 00:03:58.460
as well as the US college applicants.
56%.

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:02.700
So, do computers understand language?

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:08.400
Are you convinced? I think it would be right

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:13.769
for you to still be skeptical. I am also
still skeptical. I think we both can

00:04:13.769 --> 00:04:16.380
agree that computers do not understand
the meaning of words even though we've

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:21.529
performed these analogy tests as well as
US high school students.

00:04:21.529 --> 00:04:25.740
Let's go right to the source. Half of
my work is also on brain imaging and

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:30.470
studying what language looks like in the brain.
So let's see what word meaning looks

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:32.840
like in the brain. The way we're gonna
do this,

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:35.480
remember our computer models are just
lists of numbers.

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:36.440
It turns out that brain

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:39.860
images are also just lists of numbers so
for a particular area the list of

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:43.880
numbers represents how much activity
there is in that area. So in both cases,

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:47.690
we just have lists of numbers and we can
tell how similar the list of numbers are

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:53.690
and we can use that in order to compare
the two sources of information.

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:57.050
Essentially what we're gonna do is take
pairs of concepts, so things like "dog" and

00:04:57.050 --> 00:05:00.740
"cat," "car" and "truck" and we're gonna
compare how similar they are in using

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.979
the list of numbers from brains and
using the list of numbers from computer models.

00:05:03.979 --> 00:05:10.070
We would like to rank
these lists of pairs, and check if the

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:14.389
ranking is similar across their two data
sources. And if it's similar. then we

00:05:14.389 --> 00:05:17.090
would say that computers understand
things in the same way that the brain does.

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:24.710
So this is your brain. On the top is
the left side of your brain, and the

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:28.280
bottom is right and I'm highlighting
here just the areas of the brain that

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:31.280
are known to be involved in language
processing. So I just want you to

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:34.669
remember that there's a there's a few
little spots here, and most of them are

00:05:34.669 --> 00:05:39.830
on the left-hand side.
So now on the next slide I'm going to

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:43.729
show you essentially the results from
comparing a computer model of meaning to

00:05:43.729 --> 00:05:51.020
a human brain. Here we're going to
be colouring the brain with these colours

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:55.190
that represent how correlated those
rankings are. So if it's red or yellow

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:59.030
it's very correlated and if it's more of
a blue than it's less correlated.

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:02.090
If it's grey then it's not significant,
there's no significant relationship

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:07.940
between the computer model and the brain.
The first thing to notice is that

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:11.690
there's nothing that's grey. Which is
remarkable and interesting and not

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:15.440
something that was known before this
research was started. We thought language

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:19.520
was really relegated to these one pieces
but it turns out that language meaning

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:24.470
is all over your brain, you understand
language with your entire brain.

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:29.090
We also see the same pattern of some more red
and yellow on the left hand side but we

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:34.520
see language meaning all over the brain.
And this shows up in other studies too

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:39.500
so this is brain images captured while
people listen to podcasts. and so here we

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:42.770
Here we see if anything is black that mean
it's also not significantly related to

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:45.800
the computer model of meaning, but here
we see lots of parts of the brain are

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:50.389
not black. And that shows us that this
computer model of meaning is related to

00:06:50.389 --> 00:06:57.440
how your brain registers meaning as
you're listening to a podcast.

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:02.650
So parts of our brains do represent meaning in
the same way that computers do.

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:04.540
Computers think like us.

00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:08.400
Are you convinced?

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:09.259
Again I think you're right

00:07:09.259 --> 00:07:12.860
to remain skeptical. But it's interesting
to think about what it would take to

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:17.300
convince you that a computer understood
language.

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:20.990
Thank you very much

